Clear the request session when its commit fails

`BarbicanTransactionHook` (barbican/api/hooks.py) hands
`repositories.commit`, `rollback` and `clear` to pecan's
`TransactionHook`. Its `after()` (pecan 1.7.0 in the 2026.1 image,
1.8.0 in the 2026.2 image) commits a transactional request, or rolls
it back after an error, and only then calls `clear()`, which removes
the thread's session from the `scoped_session` registry. When the
commit raises, for example on the 10 s socket read timeout while a
Galera cluster is paused by flow control, `clear()` never runs.
SQLAlchemy (2.0.46 in the 2026.1 image, 2.0.52 in the 2026.2 image)
leaves that session in `prepared` state, the thread's next request
gets the same session, and its first statement raises
`InvalidRequestError: This session is in 'prepared' state; no further
SQL can be emitted within this transaction.` One stalled commit thus
fails two requests or more (C5C3/cobaltcore#1830).

The patch overrides `after()`: it runs pecan's `after()` in a `try`
and `repositories.clear()` in its `finally`, which covers a commit and
a rollback that raise alike. `remove()` works on a session in
`prepared` state, checked in both images. On the success path the
second clear finds no session in the registry and does nothing.
Wrapping `repositories.commit` instead would not reach the hook,
which holds the functions it was built with. The exception of the
commit propagates as before, so the failing request still answers
500. The commits inside handlers (barbican/api/controllers/orders.py
and barbican/common/quota.py call `repo.commit()` themselves) need no
change: their exception reaches pecan's `on_error`, and `after()` then
rolls back and clears.

Tests: no upstream test covers the hook, and the patch adds
barbican/tests/api/test_hooks.py. Four unit tests drive `after()` with
a mocked pecan state: a commit and a rollback that raise
`DBConnectionError` are re-raised and leave a new session that runs
`SELECT 1`, a request that is not transactional neither commits nor
rolls back and is cleared, and a transactional request without an
error commits once. A request test on `BarbicanAPIBaseTestCase` makes
the commit of `POST /secrets/` fail through a `commit` listener on the
engine, which rolls the DBAPI connection back the way a server that
lost the connection does and raises `DBConnectionError`; the
following `GET /secrets/` answers 200. Without the change to hooks.py
the GET answers 500 with the `prepared` state in the log, and the two
unit tests with a raising commit or rollback fail as well.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_barbican.sh (Test 14) asserts in
the built image that `BarbicanTransactionHook.after` with a raising
commit re-raises and leaves `repositories.get_session()` a new session
that runs `SELECT 1`.

The twins under patches/barbican/2026.1/ and patches/barbican/2026.2/
carry the same diff and differ in their Applies-to line only.

Applies to barbican 23.0.0 (2026.2).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

diff --git a/barbican/api/hooks.py b/barbican/api/hooks.py
index a6723b1e..d117a45e 100644
--- a/barbican/api/hooks.py
+++ b/barbican/api/hooks.py
@@ -49,6 +49,18 @@ class BarbicanTransactionHook(pecan.hooks.TransactionHook):
             clear=repositories.clear
         )
 
+    def after(self, state):
+        # pecan commits or rolls back and only then clears the session, so
+        # a commit that raises, for example on a socket read timeout, skips
+        # the clear. SQLAlchemy leaves the session in 'prepared' state, and
+        # this thread's next request would get the same session, in which
+        # no statement can run. Clearing here as well discards it; on the
+        # success path the second clear finds no session and does nothing.
+        try:
+            super(BarbicanTransactionHook, self).after(state)
+        finally:
+            repositories.clear()
+
 
 class NewRelicHook(pecan.hooks.PecanHook):
     def on_error(self, state, exc):
